# Line endings are pinned to LF for every text file, on every platform.
#
# WHY, MEASURED ON A REAL CLONE (27.09.2026): with the Windows default
# `core.autocrlf=true`, a fresh `git clone` checked out this repository with CRLF,
# which broke two things that matter:
#
#   1. `weights/download.sh` came out as 60 CR / 60 LF. Run under bash, the
#      first line is `set -euo pipefail\r`, and the shell dies on the stray CR.
#      So the script that exists to REPAIR the Git-LFS weight pointer did not
#      run at all on the one platform whose default caused the damage. The fix
#      for the trap was itself disabled by the trap.
#   2. `run_submission.py` came out as 8843 bytes instead of the committed
#      8629. The committed blob is still byte-identical to the organizers'
#      pristine original (sha256 a47b494a...), but the working-tree copy was not,
#      which makes the "we never touched the harness" claim unverifiable from
#      inside a Windows clone - the exact property the claim exists to assert.
#
# `eol=lf` fixes the checkout side on every platform. The blob side was already
# correct, so this changes no committed bytes: renormalising is a no-op here.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The detector weight lives in Git LFS (114,636,239 bytes > GitHub's 100 MiB
# blob limit) and must never be line-ending-processed.
weights/*.pt filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# Binary assets: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.mp4 -text
*.ico -text
*.png -text
*.jpg -text
*.jpeg -text
*.pt -text
